WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.460
How does heapsort work? Build

2
00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.480
a heap, repeatedly remove its

3
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.160
root, and repair the remaining

4
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.880
heap. Let me show the

5
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.940
tree and output together, then

6
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.140
connect this to the in-place

7
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:11.920
version.

8
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.100
With a min-heap, the root

9
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.840
is the smallest remaining key.

10
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.140
The lecture first copies each

11
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.840
extracted minimum into a separate

12
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.440
output array, from left to

13
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.500
right. That output buffer uses

14
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.720
order n extra space.

15
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.140
Remove one into output position

16
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:29.060
zero, and move the last

17
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.700
leaf, six, to the root.

18
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.480
Swap six with the smaller

19
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.740
child two, then with three;

20
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.300
the heap is valid again.

21
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.660
Remove two next and replace

22
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:39.860
it with the last leaf,

23
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.920
six. Swap six with the

24
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.880
smaller child three, then with

25
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.820
five; three is now the

26
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.060
next minimum at the root.

27
00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.440
Remove three into the next

28
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.140
output slot and put the

29
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.140
last leaf, six, at the

30
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.360
root. Swap six with the

31
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.160
smaller child four to restore

32
00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:57.540
the min-heap.

33
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.820
Remove four into output and

34
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.120
replace it with six. Swap

35
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.840
six with its only child,

36
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.320
five; the root is the

37
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.980
next smallest key again.

38
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.720
Remove five next, leaving just

39
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.540
six in the heap, so

40
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.960
no repair is needed. Finally

41
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.720
remove six; the heap is

42
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.260
empty and the separate output

43
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.200
array is sorted.

44
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.660
For the in-place version, use

45
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.380
a max-heap and put removed

46
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.900
maxima at the right end

47
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.780
of the original array. The

48
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.320
tree and array are two

49
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.600
views of the same data;

50
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.860
the green suffix reuses array

51
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:33.260
storage.

52
00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.740
Now build a max-heap from

53
00:01:35.879 --> 00:01:37.818
three, one, six, five, two,

54
00:01:37.818 --> 00:01:39.500
four. Work from the last

55
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.180
internal node upwards: six already

56
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.220
beats its child four; swap

57
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.080
one with five; then swap

58
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.860
three with six and finally

59
00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.500
with four. The root six

60
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.880
is now the largest remaining

61
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:51.400
value.

62
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:53.560
Swap the root six with

63
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.080
the last active key, three,

64
00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.860
and shrink the active prefix

65
00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.080
to five keys. Six belongs

66
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.600
to the sorted suffix, but

67
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.400
the replacement three still needs

68
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:02.800
repair.

69
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.200
Compare three with children five

70
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.340
and four, then swap with

71
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.120
the larger child five. The

72
00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.540
active heap becomes five, three,

73
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.860
four, one, two; never pull

74
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:17.320
the settled six back into

75
00:02:17.708 --> 00:02:18.180
heapify.

76
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.200
Swap five with the last

77
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.180
active key two, and shrink

78
00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.600
again. Sift two down below

79
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.560
four, leaving four, three, two,

80
00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.000
one as the active heap

81
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.620
and five, six as the

82
00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.400
sorted suffix.

83
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.580
Swap four with one and

84
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:33.820
shorten the heap to three

85
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.120
keys. Repair by swapping one

86
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.180
below three; the remaining max-heap

87
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.200
is three, one, two.

88
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:41.820
Move three to the end

89
00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.660
of that prefix, leaving two

90
00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.480
and one. Move two to

91
00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.180
its final position, and the

92
00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:48.780
singleton one needs no repair;

93
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.320
the array is now one

94
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:50.960
through six.

95
00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.580
After each completed repair, the

96
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.040
active prefix is a max-heap

97
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.860
and the suffix is sorted

98
00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.260
ascending. Every suffix key is

99
00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.420
at least as large as

100
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.400
every remaining heap key; the

101
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.960
prefix need not be sorted.

102
00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:07.240
Building costs theta n. The

103
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.440
remaining n minus one extractions

104
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.920
each cost at most order

105
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.460
log n, giving an n

106
00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:13.980
log n total upper bound

107
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.000
and a matching worst-case bound.

108
00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:18.340
Do not infer that every

109
00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.420
best case for every implementation

110
00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.980
is theta n log n.

111
00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.760
With an early-stopping repair, equal

112
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.020
keys can stop immediately; the

113
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.680
safe general guarantee is the

114
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.300
n log n upper bound.

115
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.500
Things to take note of.

116
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.540
Iterative heapify makes auxiliary space

117
00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.120
constant, while recursive repair adds

118
00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.620
a logarithmic stack. Heapsort is

119
00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.500
unstable because distant swaps can

120
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:44.760
reverse equal-key records.

121
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.060
Use a max-heap for this

122
00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.880
ascending end-placement procedure, and repair

123
00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.820
only inside the active prefix.

124
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.620
Empty and singleton inputs need

125
00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.780
no extraction loop. The sorted

126
00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.720
suffix is finished work, so

127
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.040
kindly leave it alone.
